WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.768
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-, Grammy-,

00:00:02.835 --> 00:00:04.804
and Academy Award-winning
musician, actor, and producer.

00:00:04.871 --> 00:00:06.806
His book "And Then We Rise:

00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:09.442
A Guide to Loving and Taking
Care of Self" is on sale now.

00:00:09.508 --> 00:00:11.577
Please welcome back to the show
Common, everybody.

00:00:11.644 --> 00:00:13.813
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:22.088 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:22.155 --> 00:00:30.362 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:30.429 --> 00:00:32.699
Welcome back.
-Peace, Seth, peace.

00:00:32.766 --> 00:00:35.201
-I am so -- I'm so excited
to talk to you about this book.

00:00:35.268 --> 00:00:37.003
First of all,
this is a book about wellness

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:38.771
and the way
you take care of yourself.

00:00:38.838 --> 00:00:40.172
And I will say most people,

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:41.374
if they wrote a book
about wellness,

00:00:41.441 --> 00:00:42.809
I would say,
"What right do you have?"

00:00:42.875 --> 00:00:45.511
But you, of everybody
I've ever met,

00:00:45.578 --> 00:00:47.379
always has a ton of energy

00:00:47.446 --> 00:00:49.782
and has never
looked a day older.

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:51.216
If anybody should write
this book, it's you.

00:00:51.283 --> 00:00:54.153
How did -- What gave you
the permission

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:55.721
to take this leap?

00:00:55.788 --> 00:00:59.992
-You know, a lot of my friends
at dinner would always ask me,

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:02.295
hey, they would ask me
about what I was eating,

00:01:02.362 --> 00:01:05.431
and they had a curiosity
that was there,

00:01:05.498 --> 00:01:07.767
and they were like, "Why are you
choosing to eat this way?"

00:01:07.834 --> 00:01:09.735
And then during the pandemic,

00:01:09.802 --> 00:01:11.838
a lot of my friends
called me like,

00:01:11.904 --> 00:01:13.672
"Man, what should I take?
Like, what can I take?"

00:01:13.739 --> 00:01:15.374
And I'm like,
"I'm not the doctor."

00:01:15.441 --> 00:01:16.943
I even had --
One of my best friends,

00:01:17.009 --> 00:01:18.478
who is a doctor,
called and said,

00:01:18.544 --> 00:01:20.045
"What's some things I could take

00:01:20.112 --> 00:01:22.649
to stay well
during this pandemic?"

00:01:22.715 --> 00:01:24.717
And I felt like I had

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:27.653
been blessed with the access
to these great doctors,

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.523
nutritionists,
integrative medicines,

00:01:30.589 --> 00:01:32.525
therapists, trainers.

00:01:32.591 --> 00:01:35.361
And I wanted to share
that information.

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:38.897
And I never took the position,
because I've come from a place

00:01:38.964 --> 00:01:41.367
where I didn't grow up
knowing about these things.

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:43.535
This has been a path
and a journey for me,

00:01:43.602 --> 00:01:44.570
so I just like sharing it.

00:01:44.637 --> 00:01:46.238
This book is not really --

00:01:46.305 --> 00:01:48.374
As much as I'm telling my story,
it's just about the reader.

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:50.243
It's about them
empowering themselves.

00:01:50.309 --> 00:01:53.513
So I always wanted to just
meet people where they are

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.415
and not be like, "Yo,
you need to be a vegan,"

00:01:55.481 --> 00:01:58.751
or, "You need to try
to do 12 workouts a day."

00:01:58.817 --> 00:02:01.387
It's just about taking steps
for your life to be better.

00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:03.622
-Absolutely.
My brother is a vegan.

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:06.259
And, you know, he's coming on
a few years of it now.

00:02:06.325 --> 00:02:08.961
And I will say,
we had him for Thanksgiving.

00:02:09.028 --> 00:02:11.097
-Yeah.
-And that was a thing.

00:02:11.163 --> 00:02:13.666
You know, the first time
you have a loved one

00:02:13.732 --> 00:02:16.502
who's a vegan come
to your house for a big meal,

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:18.504
I think, you know,
it takes a minute

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:20.139
to process
what exactly it means.

00:02:20.206 --> 00:02:21.808
How did you feel
like your family was

00:02:21.875 --> 00:02:23.542
when you laid that on them?

00:02:23.609 --> 00:02:25.578
-Oh, man, my mother,
my grandmother,

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:27.947
they were looking like,
"What are you talking about?

00:02:28.013 --> 00:02:29.515
You're not going to eat turkey?

00:02:29.582 --> 00:02:30.950
You're not going to eat
these greens?"

00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:32.118
Because they were so used --

00:02:32.185 --> 00:02:33.853
My whole life, I lived that way.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:36.756
So they didn't think
I was too serious about it.

00:02:36.823 --> 00:02:38.657
But once they saw
I was serious,

00:02:38.724 --> 00:02:42.028
my mother really, you know,
the next Thanksgiving,

00:02:42.095 --> 00:02:43.829
'cause I couldn't eat much
that Thanksgiving,

00:02:43.896 --> 00:02:45.598
but the next Thanksgiving,

00:02:45.665 --> 00:02:48.735
she started working on
some vegetarian meals.

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:50.869
And they weren't that tasty
at that time.

00:02:50.936 --> 00:02:53.672
But she got better.

00:02:53.739 --> 00:02:55.407
It's like I said,
things have improved.

00:02:55.474 --> 00:02:57.377
And people understood
that, like,

00:02:57.443 --> 00:02:58.611
"Yo, okay, it's vegan food,

00:02:58.677 --> 00:03:00.512
but you still can put
the soul into it,

00:03:00.579 --> 00:03:02.048
put the heart,
put the season in."

00:03:02.115 --> 00:03:03.049
So she's gotten there.

00:03:03.116 --> 00:03:05.151
I mean, this past Christmas,

00:03:05.218 --> 00:03:07.787
I mean, she cooked
some great vegan food for me.

00:03:07.854 --> 00:03:09.889
So let me give her some love
before she talk about --

00:03:09.956 --> 00:03:11.023
-Yes, absolutely.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:12.859
Well, you've always --

00:03:12.925 --> 00:03:14.760
you've always given your mom
a lot of love

00:03:14.827 --> 00:03:15.794
when you're on the show.

00:03:15.861 --> 00:03:17.663
She's, you know,
been so integral

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.498
and important to your career.
-Yeah.

00:03:19.565 --> 00:03:21.200
-She's also working
on a book right now.

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:23.102
-Yeah.
-Will you be competitive

00:03:23.169 --> 00:03:25.103
when hers come out?

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:26.272
-Well, you know, of course

00:03:26.339 --> 00:03:28.340
I want my mother's book
to do well.

00:03:28.407 --> 00:03:31.310
I want it to do great.
Maybe not better than mine.

00:03:31.377 --> 00:03:33.179
No, I'm just kidding.
I'm just kidding.

00:03:33.246 --> 00:03:35.014
I mean, I want her book to go --

00:03:35.081 --> 00:03:37.984
because her book is about
educating young people

00:03:38.051 --> 00:03:40.453
in this -- you know,
the social media age.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.288
And she's been a teacher
her whole life.

00:03:42.354 --> 00:03:45.291
Like, she knew she wanted
to do that at 5 years old.

00:03:45.358 --> 00:03:47.726
So I want -- I think
it's very important

00:03:47.793 --> 00:03:49.428
what she has to share
with people.

00:03:49.495 --> 00:03:50.963
But I am very competitive.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:54.167
I beat my mother in bowling just
over the Christmas break, too,

00:03:54.233 --> 00:03:56.368
and I never let my daughter
win a track race,

00:03:56.435 --> 00:03:58.004
even though she was
on the track team.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:01.407
When I raced against her,
I dusted her.

00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:02.541
[ Laughter ]

00:04:02.608 --> 00:04:03.742
-But at some point,

00:04:03.809 --> 00:04:05.078
you know your daughter's
going to catch up,

00:04:05.144 --> 00:04:06.846
and I just hope
you're being a good sport now.

00:04:06.912 --> 00:04:09.015
-Yeah, well, I keep
feeding her all this food

00:04:09.082 --> 00:04:10.750
so maybe she still
can't beat me.

00:04:10.817 --> 00:04:12.150
[ Laughter ]

00:04:12.217 --> 00:04:13.452
-Oh, so you're vegan.

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:14.887
You're just feeding her,
like, potato chips.

00:04:14.954 --> 00:04:17.289
-Everything. Yeah.

00:04:17.356 --> 00:04:19.091
-You've mentioned,

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:20.826
'cause this is not
the first book you've written,

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:23.396
that your mom's actually
learned some things about you

00:04:23.463 --> 00:04:25.231
that you had never told her

00:04:25.298 --> 00:04:27.199
but she's learned it
from the pages of your books.

00:04:27.266 --> 00:04:29.301
-Yeah, yeah,
one of my earlier memoirs

00:04:29.368 --> 00:04:34.139
was I revealed a lot of things
that I did as a teenager,

00:04:34.206 --> 00:04:37.310
like sneaking girls in my room

00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:40.679
and then them having to hide
under the bed

00:04:40.746 --> 00:04:43.482
when my mother's
coming to the door.

00:04:43.549 --> 00:04:46.886
And she read that book and
was like, "This is a good book,

00:04:46.952 --> 00:04:51.123
but I can't believe you did
that stuff in my house."

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:54.694
There was a lot of revelations
in that book, but you know.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:57.063
-I wonder if part of her was,
"I can't believe you did that,"

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:00.533
and another part of her was
like, "How did I never know?"

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.268
Like, "I would make
a terrible detective."

00:05:02.335 --> 00:05:05.470
-No, she definitely would,
'cause I was a slick little guy.

00:05:05.537 --> 00:05:07.673
I mean, I was a good guy,

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.242
but I had my slickness, too.

00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:11.376
-Yeah, absolutely.
Very slick.

00:05:11.443 --> 00:05:13.012
And you were always like,

00:05:13.079 --> 00:05:14.814
"I'd like a bed that's a little
bit higher off the ground."

00:05:14.881 --> 00:05:16.215
That's when she should've known.

00:05:16.282 --> 00:05:17.983
She was like,
"What's going on here?"

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:19.352
[ Laughter ]

00:05:19.419 --> 00:05:22.088
I want to ask
before we take a quick break

00:05:22.154 --> 00:05:23.956
about Make One Change.

00:05:24.023 --> 00:05:25.524
This is an initiative
you've launched.

00:05:25.591 --> 00:05:27.627
Tell us a little bit about it.
-Yeah, it's really, like,

00:05:27.693 --> 00:05:30.062
in support of the book
and the movement of the book,

00:05:30.128 --> 00:05:32.832
just saying, "Man, make
one change in your life."

00:05:32.898 --> 00:05:35.968
Like, it could be just as simple
as drinking water.

00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:39.705
My change is, like, I want
to get more rest, you know,

00:05:39.771 --> 00:05:42.542
and I realize how important
that is to health,

00:05:42.608 --> 00:05:43.675
and I hadn't been doing it.

00:05:43.742 --> 00:05:45.244
I'm doing
all these other things,

00:05:45.311 --> 00:05:46.679
but I need to get more rest.

00:05:46.745 --> 00:05:49.014
So it could be -- I mean,
I'm encouraging people

00:05:49.081 --> 00:05:50.515
just because, like I said,

00:05:50.582 --> 00:05:53.586
sometimes people think you
have to go all the way to 100

00:05:53.652 --> 00:05:56.989
to start your journey,
but it's just one step.

00:05:57.056 --> 00:05:59.092
One step got me
to where I am right now.

00:05:59.158 --> 00:06:01.227
-That's a wonderful message.
I have more to ask you about.

00:06:01.293 --> 00:06:04.097
We'll be right back with Common
after this quick break.

